            <ArticleTitle>Deep Hypothermic Circulatory Arrest in a Patient With Cold-Induced Urticaria.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>722-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2014.09.067</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-4975(14)01956-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Acquired cold urticaria is a condition characterized by the onset of pruritic hives, swelling, and occasional severe systemic reactions, during the rewarming phase, after cold exposure. Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy (PTE) is a cardiac surgical procedure where organized thrombus is excised from the pulmonary vasculature in order to improve pulmonary blood flow and relieve pressure on the right ventricle. A PTE requires institution of deep hypothermic circulatory arrest (DHCA) in order to reduce blood flow from collateral vessels during thrombus excision. We describe a case of PTE performed under DHCA in a patient with ACU.</AbstractText>
